  <h3>The Desktop vs. Mobile Cropping Conflict Explained</h3>
  <p>
    The most common issue creators encounter is how Facebook displays business page covers on desktop versus smartphones:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Desktop Rendering:</strong> Displays your cover at <strong>820 pixels wide by 312 pixels tall</strong>, forming a wide banner (~2.6:1 aspect ratio).</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile Smartphone Rendering:</strong> Displays your cover at <strong>640 pixels wide by 360 pixels tall</strong>, forming a taller frame (16:9 aspect ratio).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you upload a standard 820 × 312 pixel desktop image, Facebook stretches the height on mobile devices, cutting off the left and right sides. Conversely, if you upload a 16:9 mobile graphic, desktop monitors crop the top and bottom.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>The Universal Safe Zone Solution:</strong> Design your master canvas at <strong>820 × 360 pixels</strong> (or double resolution at <strong>1640 × 720 pixels</strong> for high-DPI sharpness). Place all essential text, logos, and focal subjects inside the central <strong>640 × 312 pixel safe zone</strong> so zero elements get cropped on any screen.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>File Formats and Compression Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>PNG Format:</strong> Best for graphics that include company logos, high-contrast text, or flat graphic illustrations to prevent compression blur around letters.</li>
    <li><strong>JPG Format:</strong> Ideal for rich photography, travel scenery, and detailed portraits where file sizes need to remain lightweight.</li>
    <li><strong>Color Profile:</strong> Always export using the standard <strong>sRGB</strong> color space to prevent washed-out colors.</li>
  </ul>

