  <h2 id="section-6">6. Best Practices for Creating Attractive Facebook Cover Photos</h2>
  <p>
    Achieving the right <strong>Facebook cover photo dimensions</strong> provides a clean foundation. To turn visitors into followers and customers, apply these proven design principles:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Center Your Value Proposition in the Safe Zone</h3>
  <p>
    Keep your most important message—whether it is a headline, a seasonal offer, or an event date—positioned centrally.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Leave at least 90 pixels of horizontal margin on both sides so mobile screens do not cut off words.</li>
    <li>Leave at least 24 pixels of vertical margin on the top and bottom so desktop letterboxing does not shave off letters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Maintain High Contrast and Typography Legibility</h3>
  <p>
    Visitors browse on screens with varying brightness levels. Text must remain readable at a glance:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use Bold Sans-Serif Fonts:</strong> Clean typefaces like Inter, Montserrat, or Roboto provide maximum clarity across mobile screens.</li>
    <li><strong>Add Contrast Overlays:</strong> If placing text over photography, apply a semi-transparent 30% to 50% dark overlay or drop shadow behind letters to ensure crisp readability.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Keep Design Elements Focused and Uncluttered</h3>
  <p>
    Avoid turning your cover photo into a crowded catalog. A banner packed with too many graphics and paragraphs confuses visitors.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Focus on <strong>one primary message</strong> or single visual focal point.</li>
    <li>Ensure your call-to-action aligns directly with the action button located beneath your cover (e.g., "Shop Now", "Sign Up", or "Join Group").</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Ensure Brand Consistency</h3>
  <p>
    Your Facebook cover photo should feel like a natural part of your brand. Use consistent brand color palettes, signature font pairings, and official logo placement so returning customers recognize your identity immediately.
  </p>

