  <h3>Mobile-First Design Workflow</h3>
  <p>
    Because the majority of social browsing happens on smartphones, begin your design process with mobile in mind. Build your core message within the central 640 × 312 pixel box, then extend background textures, scenery, and subtle gradients outward to fill the full 820 × 360 pixel desktop canvas.
  </p>

  <h3>Personal Profiles vs. Business Pages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For Personal Creators:</strong> Use horizontal portraits, travel landscapes, or creative workspace shots that convey personality, passion, and authenticity.</li>
    <li><strong>For Business Pages:</strong> Use customer social proof, product showcases, limited-time offers, or mission statements paired with clean branded graphics.</li>
    <li><strong>For Facebook Groups:</strong> Highlight community perks, member guidelines, weekly schedules, or welcoming imagery that sets an inviting tone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Keeping Cover Photos Ultra-Sharp</h3>
  <p>
    Facebook compresses uploads to conserve bandwidth. To maintain maximum sharpness:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Double your canvas dimensions to <strong>1640 × 720 pixels</strong> before uploading; Facebook downsamples high-resolution assets much cleaner than it upscales low-res files.</li>
    <li>Export in <strong>PNG format</strong> whenever your graphic features text, badges, or flat vector illustrations.</li>
    <li>Use an online <a href={toolUrl}>Facebook Cover Photo Resizer</a> to standardize dimensions and avoid repeated server-side resampling.</li>
